From: brian@jane.nrccso.com (Brian Barnhart)
Date: Tue, 15 Nov 94 14:01:20 MST
Subject: [#94-11-207] POLITICS: Chomsky on Democracy

Did anyone catch Noam Chomsky on CSPAN, discussing Democracy and the Media?

He gave a fascinating speech, rather well-referenced, on current American
statism or should I say Statism as coopted for multinational corporations.

I'm still pondering points he made, but the most troubling deal with the
'insulation of policy from politics', rendering any kind of representative
governance impotent and the people hopelessly manipulated.

I'll try to recall some issues he brought up:

Recent US support of ethnic cleansing in East Timor / Indonesia.
  Evidently, we've been the arms supplier to a genocide policy aimed
  at lucrative oil reserves in/near Indonesia.  Death tolls have
  been very high (10,000's), committed by our allies, and unreported
  in our press.  (until maybe now w/Clinton's visit there).

Operating Brazil as a satellite state.
  I have no background on this, but its not implausible.  His point
  was to compare 'progress' (std of living, etc) in our satellite
  state to that of USSRs satellites, and trying to draw conclusions
  on relative advantages of 'free market capitalism' to 'soviet
  communism' ... maybe there are none, maybe these are both Statism
  that is highly insulated from the good-of or will-of the people

Free Trade.
  Given the influence of multinationals and their operation through
  distributed production, Chomsky challenged NAFTA as an Orwellianism:
    Free: means "highly regulated", with different rules and loopholes
    Trade: means "transfer of company-internal goods across borders",
           with little actual goods-sold and payments-rendered transactions.
    Agreement: means "political coercion", given the high resistance to
               NAFTA among American, Mexican and Canadian electorates.

I'm still pondering all this ... not at all 'sold' on it, but it is 
troubling.  Not so very far removed from a-albionic's stuff, but from
a very credible source (or tell me he's a leftist whacko - maybe).

I'll try to get a transcript from CSPAN ... email me if you're
interested.

