Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.buffalo.edu> said :

> Why do you think environmentalists are power grabbers?
> Perhaps politicians become involved in environmental issues to gain
> political power, at least in Germany, but the average environmentalist
> is not seeking power in general -- certainly no more than any of us are
> to advance our viewpoints.

Except that they're seeking government regulation to enforce their
viewpoints on everyone. Most of their solutions cannot work without
coercion (or do you think that people will voluntarily pay their
"carbon tax" ?).

To a large extent the difference between mainstream environmentalists
and extropians is that the environmentalists support coercive solutions
to environmental problems, whereas we generally support market
solutions.

> Questions: Answer yes or no.

No.... Seriously, your questions are not the sort that can have simple
yes or no answers.

> 1.  Natural ecosystems and other natural life forms (e.g., not cows)

Why don't you regard cows as natural life forms ?

> are good things worth preserving, even if there is no material benefit
> and significant material loss to us from doing so.

Personally, I'd like to see most of the human race move off the earth
and let it return to a wilderness (purely for my own selfish esthetic
reasons, so I wouldn't try to coerce people into doing so), but I don't
see any reason why we should preserve things if there's no benefit
whatsoever in doing so. If there's a benefit (not neccesarily material)
then we'll do it, if there isn't then we won't (in the absence of
government coercion, of course).

> 2.  Natural ecosystems are being disrupted, species eradicated, and
> the environment polluted at rates beyond optimal.

Probably true, which is a good reason why attempts by environmentalists
to impose more government control are a bad idea, since most of these
problems are due to governments in the first place.

> 3.  There is a danger that if we do not reduce such disruption,
> eradication, and pollution soon, it will be "too late" (feel free
> to give your own interpretation of "too late").

No. I'm sure that we could create a situation which resulted in the
deaths of a large fraction of the human population, but I think we'd
have to try much harder than we are today. However, I certainly think
that we ought to start reducing the power of governments in case they
*do* do something really stupid.

> 4.  The Earth will soon have too many humans.

The Earth has too many humans for my liking (I prefer to live in areas
with very low population density), but it will be a long time before
there are more humans than it is capable of supporting (in the absence
of government restrictions).

> 5.  The Earth has too many humans already.

See above.

> 6.  Population should increase until the Earth contains as many humans
> as it can support.

I'd prefer it if it didn't, but I don't see it as a real problem since
it would encourage people to get off the planet and/or uploaded instead
(again, in the absence of government opposition). I'm generally opposed
to coercive restrictions on population growth, but I don't see much
point in deliberately trying to dramatically *increase* the population
either.

> 7.  Everyone should be content to live in a city.  Country living is a
> luxury which takes too many resources per individual.

People should live wherever they prefer, and in any case country living
may well enable you to live with far less resources than in a city
(e.g. growing your own food, generating your own power, etc). 

Now, if we were to dramatically increase the population then there
might be no alternative, since we'd pretty much have to move to closed
ecosystems. But again, that would merely create pressure to get off of
this planet and/or uploaded.

> 8.  The Earth is ours, and we have no obligations to other species.

We have no obligation to anyone. We might decide that we want to keep
other species around for various reasons, but I don't see that we
can have any "obligation" to do so unless you're presupposing some
kind of creator who will beat us up if we don't do what we're
supposed to do.

> 9.  Intellect is the greatest virtue, and creatures with greater intellect
> should eventually replace all creatures of lesser intellect.

Creatures of greater intellect *will* eventually replace creatures of
lesser intellect. They probably won't replace all of them, and they'll
probably keep some around as pets, but there's really no question that
they will take over if they want to.

		Mark

