		  Partners To Develop Water-based Fuels
 		  -------------------------------------

      From Research & Development, October, 1994, Vol. 36, No. 1, Pg 12
      Author:  Jim Wilson

    The world's largest manufacturer of industrial moving equipment 
    Caterpillar Inc., Peoria, Ill., has entered into a joint venture with
    A-55 Limited Partnership of Reno, Nev., to further develop and
    commercialize a new technology that lets internal combustion engines
    operate on a mixture of water and carbon-based fuel. 

    Invented by Rudolf Gunnerman, A-55's chief executive officer, the fuel 
    for gasoline engines is 55% water and 45% diesel fuel.  A-55 fuels also
    contain a surfactant that permits the carbon-based components to mix
    with water. 

    Another part of the A-55 technology involves modifying gasoline and 
    diesel engines so they can burn the special fuels.  A small amount of
    nickel must be added to each cylinder.  Acting as a catalyst, the metal
    accelerates the breakdown of water into its constituent hydrogen and
    oxygen atoms. 

    Also, the microcomputers controlling fuel injection and ignition in 
    gasoline engines need to be replaced with a new chip programmed to
    accommodate A-55's combustion characteristics. 

    Gunnerman has successfully tested A-55 on a bus.  next comes a Corvette.

    Caterpillar has not yet said how it plans to market the technology.


